# Performance, weight and balance: PHAK (FAA-H-8083-25C) chapters 10 and 11; Aircraft Weight and Balance Handbook (FAA-H-8083-1B). ACS area I.F. Every chart is replaced by numbers given in the stem.
ITEMS = [
    # ---------- Practice Test 1 ----------
    S(1, 2, "Pressure altitude", "Field elevation is 1,380 feet and the altimeter setting is 30.12. What is the pressure altitude?",
      ["1,180 feet", "1,580 feet", "1,380 feet"],
      "Pressure altitude = field elevation + (29.92 - altimeter setting) x 1,000. 29.92 - 30.12 = -0.20, so 1,380 - 200 = 1,180 feet."),
    S(1, 3, "Density altitude", "The pressure altitude is 4,000 feet and the outside air temperature is 25 degrees C. Using the rule of about 120 feet per degree C above standard, what is the approximate density altitude?",
      ["6,200 feet", "4,000 feet", "7,000 feet"],
      "Standard temperature at 4,000 feet is 15 - (2 x 4) = 7 degrees C. The air is 18 degrees warmer, and 18 x 120 = 2,160 feet. Density altitude is about 4,000 + 2,160, or 6,200 feet."),
    S(1, 2, "Density altitude", "Which combination gives the highest density altitude and the poorest airplane performance?",
      ["High elevation, high temperature and high humidity",
       "High elevation, low temperature and low humidity",
       "Low elevation, high temperature and low humidity"],
      "Density altitude rises with elevation, temperature and humidity, because each makes the air less dense. Water vapor is lighter than dry air, so humid air reduces performance too."),
    S(1, 3, "Center of gravity", "Empty weight is 1,500 pounds with a moment of 57,000 pound-inches. You add 340 pounds of people at 37.0 inches, 30 gallons of fuel at 48.0 inches and 50 pounds of baggage at 95.0 inches. Where is the center of gravity?",
      ["About 40.1 inches aft of datum", "About 38.5 inches aft of datum", "About 41.6 inches aft of datum"],
      "Fuel weighs 30 x 6 = 180 pounds. Moments: 57,000 + (340 x 37) 12,580 + (180 x 48) 8,640 + (50 x 95) 4,750 = 82,970. Weight: 1,500 + 340 + 180 + 50 = 2,070. CG = 82,970 / 2,070, about 40.1 inches."),
    S(1, 2, "Takeoff performance", "How does a headwind affect takeoff performance?",
      ["It shortens the ground roll and steepens the climb gradient over the ground",
       "It lengthens the ground roll but steepens the climb gradient over the ground",
       "It has no effect, because the airplane lifts off at the same indicated airspeed"],
      "The airplane lifts off at the same indicated airspeed, but a headwind means a lower groundspeed at liftoff, so the ground roll is shorter and the climb is steeper relative to the ground."),
    S(1, 1, "Climb speeds", "Which speed gives the greatest altitude gain over the shortest horizontal distance, for clearing an obstacle after takeoff?",
      ["VX", "VY", "VA"],
      "VX, the best angle of climb speed, gives the most altitude per distance. VY, the best rate of climb speed, gives the most altitude per unit of time."),
    S(1, 2, "Weight shift", "An airplane weighs 2,400 pounds. How much weight must be moved 60 inches aft to move the CG aft by 2.0 inches?",
      ["80 pounds", "40 pounds", "120 pounds"],
      "Weight moved / total weight = CG change / distance moved. Weight moved = 2,400 x 2.0 / 60 = 80 pounds."),
    # ---------- Practice Test 2 ----------
    S(2, 1, "Standard weights", "For weight and balance, what is the standard weight of aviation gasoline?",
      ["6 pounds per gallon", "7.5 pounds per gallon", "8.35 pounds per gallon"],
      "Avgas is computed at 6 pounds per US gallon. Oil is 7.5 pounds per gallon and water is about 8.35 pounds per gallon."),
    S(2, 3, "Crosswind component", "You plan to land on runway 17 with a reported wind from 210 degrees at 20 knots. About what is the crosswind component?",
      ["13 knots", "15 knots", "7 knots"],
      "The wind is 40 degrees off the runway heading of 170. Crosswind = 20 x sin 40 (about 0.64) = about 13 knots. The headwind component is 20 x cos 40, about 15 knots."),
    S(2, 2, "Useful load", "What is useful load?",
      ["The maximum weight minus the basic empty weight",
       "The weight of the pilot, passengers and baggage only",
       "The basic empty weight plus the weight of usable fuel"],
      "Useful load is the difference between maximum weight and basic empty weight. It covers pilot, passengers, baggage and usable fuel (and oil if not included in empty weight)."),
    S(2, 2, "Overweight", "Which is a result of operating an airplane above its maximum weight?",
      ["A higher stall speed and a longer takeoff run",
       "A lower stall speed and a shorter takeoff run",
       "A higher cruise speed and a steeper climb"],
      "Extra weight requires more lift, so the airplane stalls at a higher speed, takes a longer runway, climbs more slowly and can exceed structural limits in turbulence."),
    S(2, 3, "Weight reduction", "Your planned takeoff weight is 2,550 pounds and the maximum is 2,450 pounds. If you remove only fuel, about how many gallons must you remove?",
      ["17 gallons", "10 gallons", "25 gallons"],
      "You must remove 100 pounds. At 6 pounds per gallon that is 100 / 6, about 16.7, so about 17 gallons. Then check that the remaining fuel still covers the flight and the reserve."),
    S(2, 2, "Landing performance", "How does a high density altitude affect landing?",
      ["The true airspeed is higher for the same indicated airspeed, so the landing roll is longer",
       "The indicated approach speed must be raised to stay above the higher stall speed",
       "The true airspeed is lower for the same indicated airspeed, so the landing roll is shorter"],
      "Indicated stall and approach speeds stay the same, but in thin air the true airspeed and groundspeed are higher, which lengthens the landing distance."),
    S(2, 2, "Glide distance", "An airplane with a glide ratio of 9 to 1 has an engine failure at 6,000 feet AGL. In still air, about how far can it glide?",
      ["About 9 nautical miles", "About 6 nautical miles", "About 15 nautical miles"],
      "9 x 6,000 feet = 54,000 feet. A nautical mile is about 6,076 feet, so 54,000 / 6,076 is about 8.9 nautical miles."),
    # ---------- Drill only ----------
    S(0, 1, "Moments", "A 170-pound passenger sits 90 inches aft of the datum. What is the moment?",
      ["15,300 pound-inches", "1,530 pound-inches", "26,000 pound-inches"],
      "Moment = weight x arm = 170 x 90 = 15,300 pound-inches."),
    S(0, 2, "Center of gravity", "How is the center of gravity found from a loading calculation?",
      ["Total moment divided by total weight",
       "Total weight divided by total moment",
       "Total arm multiplied by total weight"],
      "Add each item's weight and moment. The CG, measured in inches from the datum, is the total moment divided by the total weight."),
    S(0, 2, "Empty weight", "Under the GAMA standard used in most modern POHs, basic empty weight includes",
      ["unusable fuel and full engine oil", "usable fuel and the pilot's weight", "only the airframe, engine and fixed equipment"],
      "Basic empty weight is the standard empty weight plus optional equipment. Under GAMA it includes unusable fuel, full operating fluids and full oil."),
    S(0, 2, "Density altitude", "How does high humidity affect takeoff performance?",
      ["It reduces it, because moist air is less dense",
       "It improves it, because moist air is more dense",
       "It has no effect on piston engine airplanes"],
      "Water vapor weighs less than the nitrogen and oxygen it replaces, so humid air is less dense. Engine power, propeller thrust and wing lift all drop."),
    S(0, 2, "Runway surface", "How does taking off from a grass runway with tall wet grass affect the takeoff?",
      ["It increases the takeoff distance", "It decreases the takeoff distance", "It does not change the takeoff distance"],
      "Grass, soft ground, snow or standing water add rolling resistance, so the airplane accelerates more slowly and the ground roll is longer. Many POHs give a correction for grass."),
    S(0, 2, "Runway slope", "Compared with a level runway, taking off uphill",
      ["lengthens the takeoff roll", "shortens the takeoff roll", "does not change the takeoff roll"],
      "On an upslope part of the airplane's weight acts against the thrust, slowing acceleration and lengthening the takeoff roll. A downhill landing similarly lengthens the landing roll."),
    S(0, 3, "Pressure altitude", "Field elevation is 3,250 feet and the altimeter setting is 29.72. What is the pressure altitude?",
      ["3,450 feet", "3,050 feet", "3,250 feet"],
      "29.92 - 29.72 = 0.20, and 0.20 x 1,000 = 200 feet. Pressure altitude is 3,250 + 200 = 3,450 feet. A setting below 29.92 makes pressure altitude higher than field elevation."),
    S(0, 2, "Fuel planning", "An airplane burns 8.5 gallons per hour. How much fuel is used in a flight of 2 hours 15 minutes?",
      ["About 19.1 gallons", "About 17.0 gallons", "About 21.3 gallons"],
      "2 hours 15 minutes is 2.25 hours. 8.5 x 2.25 = 19.125 gallons, about 19.1 gallons, before adding taxi, climb and the required reserve."),
    S(0, 3, "Crosswind component", "Runway 31 is in use and the wind is from 340 degrees at 18 knots. About what is the crosswind component?",
      ["9 knots", "16 knots", "18 knots"],
      "The wind is 30 degrees off the runway heading of 310. Crosswind = 18 x sin 30 (0.5) = 9 knots. The headwind component is 18 x cos 30, about 16 knots."),
    S(0, 2, "Aft CG", "Loading an airplane so the CG is aft of its limit makes it",
      ["less stable and harder to recover from a stall or spin",
       "more stable and easier to recover from a stall or spin",
       "nose heavy, so it needs more back pressure to flare"],
      "An aft CG shortens the tail's lever arm for recovery, so the airplane is less stable in pitch and stall or spin recovery may be difficult or impossible."),
]
